<%# The mark, drawn into the page rather than fetched as an image.

    It was an <img src="/icons/logo.svg"> and the wordmark was therefore set in whatever
    sans the reader's system happened to supply, because an SVG behind an <img> is a
    separate document: it gets no webfont, no stylesheet, and on this page default-src
    'none' would refuse it anything it went looking for. So the one place the brand states
    its own name was the one place it could not do it in the brand's own face.

    Outlining the glyphs would have kept the <img> and cost about 2.2 KB of path data for
    the ten distinct letters, against a file that is meant to stay well under 3 KB because
    it is part of the first paint of a page somebody is sitting in front of mid-handshake.
    Inline, the text is text: it takes Blonde Sans off the page's own stylesheet, which the
    consent CSP already allows with font-src 'self', and costs nothing to fetch at all
    because there is no second document to fetch.

    Two plates rather than four. Four was a stack and the stack is what made the tilts read
    as strata; two says the same thing more quietly and leaves the mark legible at the
    224px this is actually drawn at. The bottom one is flat because it is still lying where
    it was put, and the top one is tilted and offset because everything above the bottom
    plate has moved. That is the whole idea and it survives losing half the plates.

    The holes are real holes, cut with fill-rule evenodd rather than painted in the page's
    background colour, so the mark keeps working on any surface it is ever put on. A plate
    without one reads as a disc, and a disc is the target this mark stopped being. %>
<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 310 72" role="img"
     aria-labelledby="tectonic-logo-title" class="<%= classes %>">
  <title id="tectonic-logo-title">tectonic plates</title>
  <path fill-rule="evenodd" fill="#075985"
        d="M4 52a28 10 0 1 0 56 0a28 10 0 1 0 -56 0ZM24.5 52a7.5 2.7 0 1 0 15 0a7.5 2.7 0 1 0 -15 0Z" />
  <path fill-rule="evenodd" fill="#84cc16" transform="rotate(-8 40 26)"
        d="M13 26a27 10 0 1 0 54 0a27 10 0 1 0 -54 0ZM32.8 26a7.2 2.6 0 1 0 14.4 0a7.2 2.6 0 1 0 -14.4 0Z" />
  <%# `brand` is the same class every heading on the site takes, so the mark cannot drift
      from the rest of the type: change the face there and it changes here. The fallback in
      that rule is a plain sans, and Blonde Sans is narrower than most of them -- the run is
      about 170px of the 222 there is room for at this size, so even a fallback a fifth
      wider still lands inside the viewBox rather than being clipped by it. %>
  <text x="88" y="52" class="brand" font-size="34" fill="#075985">tectonic plates</text>
</svg>
